WEBVTT

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.325
Successful local inference depends on a few core terms.

00:00:03.325 --> 00:00:06.300
A model file contains the data needed to run a trained model,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.445
commonly in GGUF format for llama.cpp workflows.

00:00:09.445 --> 00:00:13.530
The tokenizer converts text into tokens the model can process and converts generated

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.960
tokens back into text.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.690
A prompt is the input you provide, while the context window is the amount of prompt,

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.640
conversation history, and supporting text the model can consider during a run.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.720
Prompt processing and token generation are two different phases.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.968
Prompt processing is the work of reading and preparing the input context.

00:00:28.968 --> 00:00:31.620
Token generation is the step-by-step creation of output.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.965
A setup may process short prompts quickly but slow down with long context.

00:00:34.965 --> 00:00:38.895
It may load a model successfully but generate slowly because the chosen model,

00:00:38.895 --> 00:00:42.560
quantization, hardware, or runtime settings are not well matched.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.570
Sampling settings shape the output.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.190
Temperature and related controls affect how deterministic, varied,

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.160
or creative the result feels.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.610
Output length limits prevent runaway responses.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:54.345
Stop tokens help the runtime know when to end a completion.

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:56.400
These settings do not make a weak model strong,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.860
but they can make a suitable model behave poorly if configured carelessly.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:04.480
CPU inference, GPU acceleration, and quantization are practical operating choices.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.345
CPU inference can be simple and portable but slower for larger models.

00:01:08.345 --> 00:01:12.840
GPU acceleration can improve speed when supported by hardware and runtime configuration.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.690
Quantization reduces memory requirements and file size,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.240
often with quality or behavior tradeoffs.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.760
Local inference works best when model, runtime, hardware,

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.840
and configuration are treated as one system.
